<span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">94.8</span></span>
<span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.4.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">97.0</span>
<span id="S4.T3.5.1.1.1.1.1.1.4.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">112</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Impact of retained tokens on performance.</span>
One highlight of LightVLA is its ability to adaptively distinguish useful tokens. To validate this ability, we manipulate the token pruning scheme of LightVLA in the following ways. First, after LightVLA has retained <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, we supplement another <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> random tokens into the pruned set, resulting in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens sent into the LLM. This manipulation validates if any useful tokens have been omitted by LightVLA. Second, after LightVLA has retained <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, we randomly discard <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the tokens from the pruned set, resulting in <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.9k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.9k</annotation></semantics></math> tokens sent into the LLM. This manipulation tests if any useless tokens have been retained by LightVLA. The results shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="TABLE IV ‣ IV-D Ablation Study ‣ IV Experiments ‣ The Better You Learn, The Smarter You Prune: Towards Efficient Vision-language-action Models via Differentiable Token Pruning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> demonstrate that any manipulation to the token pruning scheme will result in performance degradation, proving LightVLA’s ability to retain only useful tokens and discard useless tokens.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
